\title{enum2x2: every 2x2 table compatible with a published agreement statistic}
\description{
A study comparing two binary criteria on one population computes its numbers
from a 2x2 table, then publishes the numbers and not the table. The quantity
a reader wants --- how many cases the two criteria classify differently, and
in which direction --- is gone. It can often be recovered, because the same
reports print the sample size, both marginal totals and a statistic such as
kappa, and together those leave a few integer tables, often just one.
}
\details{
[recover2x2()] takes one comparison and returns every integer table whose
statistics round back to the digits the source printed. [enum.2x2()] is the
data frame form, shaped after [metafor::conv.2x2()]. The arithmetic
underneath is exposed on its own: [exact_interval()] and [rounding_interval()]
for what a printed figure stands for, [counts_rounding_to()] for the counts a
printed marginal admits, and [kappa2x2()], [exact_kappa()], [kappa_min()] and
[kappa_max()] for the statistic and the bounds its marginals impose.

Rounding is the whole problem. With exact inputs the algebra is one line: for
positive rates \eqn{p_A} and \eqn{p_B} on \eqn{N} observations,
\eqn{p_e = p_A p_B + (1 - p_A)(1 - p_B)}, \eqn{p_o = \kappa(1 - p_e) + p_e},
and \eqn{p_{11} = (p_o + p_A + p_B - 1)/2}. Published inputs are rounded, so
each printed figure is an interval and the inputs identify a set of integer
tables rather than one. This package walks that set.
}
\section{Cell names}{

Cell names follow [metafor::conv.2x2()]: `ai` is positive on both criteria,
`bi` positive on the first and negative on the second, `ci` negative on the
first and positive on the second, `di` negative on both. The marginals are
`n1i = ai + bi` and `n2i = ai + ci`, and `ni = ai + bi + ci + di`.
}

\section{Exact arithmetic}{

A printed figure is a decimal string and a table's statistic is a ratio of
integers, so both are rational and membership is decided exactly. The
comparison is done by integer cross-multiplication on doubles, which hold
every integer below 2^53 exactly, with base-10^7 limb arithmetic where a
cross-product would exceed that. There is no tolerance parameter and no
dependency.
}

